The "%" custom specifier

A percent sign (%) in a format string causes a number to be multiplied by 100 before it is formatted. The localized percent symbol is inserted in the number at the location where the % appears in the format string. The percent character used is defined by the System.Globalization.NumberFormatInfo.PercentSymbol property of the current System.Globalization.NumberFormatInfo object.

The following example defines several custom format strings that include the "%" custom specifier.

    double value = .086;
    Console.WriteLine(value.ToString("#0.##%", CultureInfo.InvariantCulture));
    Console.WriteLine(String.Format(CultureInfo.InvariantCulture,
                                    "{0:#0.##%}", value));
    // Displays 8.6%

    Dim value As Double = .086
    Console.WriteLine(value.ToString("#0.##%", CultureInfo.InvariantCulture))
    Console.WriteLine(String.Format(CultureInfo.InvariantCulture,
                                    "{0:#0.##%}", value))
    ' Displays 8.6%

