Walkthrough: Using BatchBlock and BatchedJoinBlock to Improve Efficiency

The TPL Dataflow Library provides the [System.Threading.Tasks.Dataflow.BatchBlock1?displayProperty=nameWithType](xref:System.Threading.Tasks.Dataflow.BatchBlock1?displayProperty=nameWithType) and [System.Threading.Tasks.Dataflow.BatchedJoinBlock2?displayProperty=nameWithType](xref:System.Threading.Tasks.Dataflow.BatchedJoinBlock2?displayProperty=nameWithType) classes so that you can receive and buffer data from one or more sources and then propagate out that buffered data as one collection. This batching mechanism is useful when you collect data from one or more sources and then process multiple data elements as a batch. For example, consider an application that uses dataflow to insert records into a database. This operation can be more efficient if multiple items are inserted at the same time instead of one at a time sequentially. This document describes how to use the [System.Threading.Tasks.Dataflow.BatchBlock1](xref:System.Threading.Tasks.Dataflow.BatchBlock1) class to improve the efficiency of such database insert operations. It also describes how to use the [System.Threading.Tasks.Dataflow.BatchedJoinBlock2](xref:System.Threading.Tasks.Dataflow.BatchedJoinBlock2) class to capture both the results and any exceptions that occur when the program reads from a database.

Note

The TPL dataflow library (the System.Threading.Tasks.Dataflow namespace) is included in .NET 6 and later versions. For .NET Framework and .NET Standard projects, you need to install the 📦 System.Threading.Tasks.Dataflow NuGet package.

