The System.Timers.Timer class

Another timer that can be used in a multithreaded environment is System.Timers.Timer?displayProperty=nameWithType that by default raises an event on a System.Threading.ThreadPool thread.

When you create a System.Timers.Timer?displayProperty=nameWithType object, you may specify the time interval in which to raise an System.Timers.Timer.Elapsed event. Use the System.Timers.Timer.Enabled property to indicate if a timer should raise an System.Timers.Timer.Elapsed event. If you need an System.Timers.Timer.Elapsed event to be raised only once after the specified interval has elapsed, set System.Timers.Timer.AutoReset to false. The default value of the System.Timers.Timer.AutoReset property is true, which means that an System.Timers.Timer.Elapsed event is raised regularly at the interval defined by the System.Timers.Timer.Interval property.

For more information and examples, see System.Timers.Timer?displayProperty=nameWithType.

The System.Threading.PeriodicTimer class

The System.Threading.PeriodicTimer?displayProperty=nameWithType class enables you to await individual ticks of a specified interval, performing work after calling System.Threading.PeriodicTimer.WaitForNextTickAsync*?displayProperty=nameWithType.

When you create a System.Threading.PeriodicTimer?displayProperty=nameWithType object, you specify a System.TimeSpan that determines the length of time between each tick of the timer. Instead of passing a callback or setting an event handler as in the previous timer classes, you perform work directly in scope, awaiting System.Threading.PeriodicTimer.WaitForNextTickAsync* to advance the timer by the specified interval.

The System.Threading.PeriodicTimer.WaitForNextTickAsync* method returns a ValueTask<bool>; true upon successful firing of the timer, and false when the timer has been canceled by calling System.Threading.PeriodicTimer.Dispose*?displayProperty=nameWithType. System.Threading.PeriodicTimer.WaitForNextTickAsync* optionally accepts a System.Threading.CancellationToken, which results in a System.Threading.Tasks.TaskCanceledException when a cancellation has been requested.

For more information, see System.Threading.PeriodicTimer?displayProperty=nameWithType.

